  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nToday through tonight...\n\nCurrent KIND Radar imagery depicts quiet weather conditions across \ncentral Indiana as weak surface ridging remains in place. Upstream \nconvection is ongoing across portions of MO/IL with an approaching \nsystem. Look for the disturbance along with an associated cold front \nto move in later today promoting scattered thunderstorm development, \nprimarily from mid-morning into the afternoon hours. Moderate to \nstrong destabilization ahead of the cold front and strengthening \ndeep-layer wind shear supports the potential for severe \nthunderstorms. \n\nOrganized convection and supercells are expected with \neffective shear values around 35-50 kt, mainly over southeast \nportions of central Indiana as the cold front will likely move \nthrough the northwest half of the forecast area before sufficient \ndestabilization occurs. Damaging wind gusts are the primary hazard, \nbut large hail, localized flooding, and an isolated tornado threat \nalso appear possible. Forecast soundings depict gradually veering \nlow-level hodographs during the afternoon hours while a LLJ pushes \nthrough supporting the isolated tornado threat. \n\nLook for the cold front and associated convection to quickly move \nout by the early evening hours. Cold air advection from surface high \npressure building in behind the departing front will then promote \nquiet weather conditions. Expect cooler temperatures in the low to \nmid 60s overnight. \n\nWednesday through Friday night...\n\nAnother embedded shortwave and associated cold front will advect \neven cooler-drier air into the area late Tuesday night into \nWednesday. Stronger surface high pressure building in favors quiet \nweather conditions during the period. Expect an upper wave \napproaching from the west on Friday to result in increasing mid-high \nclouds. Most guidance suggest subsidence induced dry air from the \nsurface high should inhibit any precipitation chances from the \nweakening disturbance so will keep the forecast dry. Look for \ntemperatures to remain cooler than normal with highs generally in \nthe 70s to near 80F.  \n\nSaturday through early next week...\n\nLong range guidance depicts weak surface high pressure remaining the \ndominant weather feature for much of the weekend providing mostly \nquiet weather conditions. Aloft, northwesterly flow should allow for \na few shortwaves to pass through the region. These disturbances \nalong with deeper moisture advecting into central Indiana will \nreturn low rain chances to the forecast at times late Sunday into \nearly next week. \n\nDiverging model solutions leads to lower confidence in exact details \ntowards the end of the period. Widespread severe weather is not \nexpected at this time, but ensemble guidance suggest at least \nmoderate destabilization both Sunday and Monday afternoon. This \nalong with enhancement of mid-upper level flow from the passing \nshortwaves could support a threat for severe weather. Uncertainty \nwill remain high until models become better aligned. \n\nWarm air advection ahead of the aforementioned disturbances should \nwarm temperatures well into the 80s to possibly near 90F in some \nspots Sunday and Monday.",
